		"nextNodeId": 72,
		"nodes": {
			"0": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "SHAPES\n\nHey there trooper! You've made it through the Fundamentals tutorials and are ready to tackle the basic tutorials.\n\nWe are starting out with shapes because they are very easy to use and directly give us something visually to work with.\n\nSo go ahead, sink your teeth into this tutorial!\nMake sure that after you finish you take some time to practice what you've learned before moving on."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 238.119415283203125,
					"width": 444.326904296875,
					"x": -21.698028564453125,
					"y": 145.8255157470703125
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffd0c117",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"1": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "GENERAL CONCEPT\n\nShape 2D is a special kind of type in Wire.\nIn essence the Shape 2D that comes out of these nodes are complex mathematical operations (known as SDF's) that your GPU can use to draw them onto your screen.\nThis is important to know when working with shapes because it prevents you from thinking in pixels.\n\nShapes can be used to create all kinds of useful base images, masks, UV's and animations .\nYou can use the shapes as they come like the Circle, Pentagon and Rhombus below.\nAlternatively, they can be combined and distorted in all sorts of fun ways like the little patch below.\n\nBonus: You will frequently see the Hub node in the tutorials. \nThis is a node that does nothing to the signal but can be used to organise your patches.\nIn the patch below it prevents the cord from the Rectangle node to go underneath the Union node on its way to the Subtract node."
					}
				},
